{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfe2tPLix2Lcmgf5wHwhT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I submitted an equal-weight portfolio of 141 industry momentum signals: the 21-day and 63-day time-series momentum signals plus the 5/21 moving-average crossover, across all 47 industries. Its training Sharpe (1970–1999) is 0.48, but its single validation backtest (2000–2012) came back **FAIL**.\n\n**How I chose it:**\n- **Individual signals don't hold up.** When I split the training period in half, a signal's Sharpe in the first half predicted its Sharpe in the second half slightly *negatively* (correlation −0.16). Picking the best single signals would mostly be picking noise.\n- **Whole families are steadier.** Short- and medium-horizon momentum (21-day, 63-day and the 5/21 crossover) had positive average Sharpe in both halves. The longer horizons (126-day, 252-day, 50/200) had positive average Sharpe in the first half but negative in the second. Even the chosen mix had a weak middle third of the training period (Sharpe −0.12).\n\n**Why I used only one backtest:** The instructions gave two different limits (200 calls and up to 5,000), and the research stopped at the first PASS. Running many variants until one passes would mean tuning to the 2000–2012 data. The resulting PASS would then mostly reflect how many tries it took, not a real effect. So I fixed the portfolio using only the training data, tested it once, and submitted it unchanged after the FAIL. The real out-of-sample edge of these signals looks modest, and this result reflects that.\n\nI equally weighted the 21-day and 63-day hedged-momentum signals and the 5/21 moving-average crossover across all 47 industries. I chose it because these families had positive average Sharpe in both halves of the 1970–1999 period, giving a 0.48 training Sharpe, but its single 2000–2012 validation check came back FAIL."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_